# GitHub workflow

## Branches

- **Always create a branch** for any feature, fix, or test task. One per logical unit of work like the PR. Never commit directly to `main`.
- **Naming:** `<tool-or-harness>/<short-descriptor>` using kebab-case. **Tool or harness** is the primary system assisting with the branch (use a short, consistent lowercase slug): e.g. `cursor/…` (Cursor), `claude/…` (Claude Code), `codex/…`, or another you standardize for this repo. The **descriptor** must reflect the actual work, not the chat title (e.g. `cursor/bde-form-tests`, not `cursor/review-ad-campaign-abc123`).
- For work with no agent assist, `manual/…` is fine.

## Pull requests

- **Always open a PR** when a feature, fix, or test task is complete. Use the **GitHub CLI** (`gh`) — e.g. `gh pr create`.
- **OpenSpec `/opsx:apply`:** Commit/push/PR timing lives in the active schema’s `apply.instruction` (see `openspec/schemas/*/schema.yaml`) and `skills/openspec-apply-change/SKILL.md` step 8 — not duplicated here.
- **One PR per logical unit of work.** Don't bundle unrelated changes.
- **Never merge or approve the PR.** User reviews CI checks and the Vercel preview, and always makes the final merge decision. Agents must not run `gh pr merge` or approve reviews.
- **Never have two PRs open for the same work item.** If a PR exists for the current task, push additional fixes to that branch rather than opening a new PR. Keep iterating on the existing PR until all checks pass.
- **PR description** must have **both**, under their own headings: (a) a `## Summary` list of **all** the code changes in the PR — complete, not highlights — and (b) a `## Founder intent` summary of Katy's original intent for the change, in her own framing as it was explained to you: the *why*, not just the *what*. Keep them separate; a single "what changed and why" list collapses the two and the *why* is the half that gets dropped. Follow-up commits pushed after review get added to the Summary list, so the PR stays a complete record.
- **A `## Test plan` section is welcome** whenever there is something real to say about verification — automated tests the PR implements, or how a change with no test harness (a workflow step, a shell script) was actually exercised. Say plainly which it is. What it must not be is filler: if nothing was verified, leave the section out rather than writing one that says so.
- **Tasks stay true.** Checkboxes in `openspec/changes/*/tasks.md` are claims, and stale claims cost more than empty ones. Any PR that advances, verifies, or supersedes work tracked in an active change must update that change's `tasks.md` **in the same PR**: tick `[x]` only with a dated receipt (PR number, live IDs, query/screenshot evidence), use `[~]` for partial or superseded items with a note saying what remains. This applies even when the session never touches `openspec/` — app-code PRs and verification sessions are where drift happens. When Katy reports launch/ops actions done outside a session (dashboard clicks, env keys set), verify what's checkable (Stripe, Supabase, deploy) and record the receipt in `tasks.md` then.
- **Founder intent is required on every PR**, chores and admin included — not only OpenSpec work. This keeps Katy's words in the durable GitHub record so project history ("tell-the-story"-style ledgers) can be derived from PRs instead of reconstructed by archaeology, and the PRs that most need it are often the small ones nobody writes a change doc for.
- **Keep Founder intent to one paragraph, six sentences at the absolute most.** Katy writes terse. A long section is not a fuller record — it is the agent's voice crowding hers out, and it makes the one part of the PR that is supposed to be *hers* read like everything else.
- **Quote her; bias hard against paraphrasing.** Pull her actual words from the session or from the change's Human anchor and quote them verbatim. Paraphrase only the parts she never said outright, and prefer a short quote plus a line of context over a smooth summary in your own words — the summary is the thing that decays into unusable history.
- **Founder intent carries her words and reasons only.** Findings, diagnosis, and what the investigation turned up belong in `## Summary` or `## Notes`. Once the section is explaining what *you* discovered, it has stopped being founder intent — this is the failure mode to watch for, because the analysis is usually the part you most want to write down.
- **Embed proposed-design screenshots for UI changes.** When a change has a Figma proposed frame (see `rules/figma.mdc`), include the screenshot(s) **inline in the PR body**, not just a Figma link. Because Figma's screenshot URLs are short-lived and branch-relative URLs break when the branch is deleted on merge, commit the PNG into the change's assets (`openspec/changes/<id>/assets/proposed-*.png`) and reference it by **commit-SHA** raw URL so it survives merge: `![…](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/<owner>/<repo>/<commit-sha>/openspec/changes/<id>/assets/<file>.png)`. Capture with the Figma MCP `get_screenshot` (curl the short-lived URL to the asset path), commit, then build the body against that commit's SHA.

### Single ready-for-review PR until merge (default)

- Prefer **one active branch + one PR** per ongoing effort. Keep **adding commits to that branch** and **updating the same PR** until user approves and merges. Avoid opening many parallel branches/PRs for follow-ups in the same workstream.
- Open PRs **ready for review, not draft**, so the automated Copilot review kicks off immediately (`gh pr create` without `--draft`). If a PR was opened as a draft, mark it ready (`gh pr ready`).
- Start a **new** branch + PR only after the previous one is merged, or when the work is **clearly unrelated** (different experiment, different product surface).
- Push additional commits to the same branch to refresh the existing PR.

### Copilot review loop

- After opening a PR (or pushing substantive new commits to one), **wait for Copilot's automated review** and check for its comments before ending the task — it usually posts within a few minutes (`gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/NUMBER/comments`).
- **Address every actionable comment**: push fixes to the same branch, then **reply to each comment** saying what changed — or why no change was needed. Never leave a Copilot comment unanswered.
- If a session finds an open PR with unanswered Copilot comments from an earlier session, address them the same way.
- The user still reviews and makes the final merge decision.

